I expanded the Discover filters and pushed the change to `main` (commit `e1ea72e`).

**New filter options**
- **Release status** — Airing now / Finished / Upcoming / On hiatus / Cancelled
- **Source material** — Original, Manga, Light novel, Visual novel, Video game, Novel, Doujinshi, Other
- **Country of origin** — Japan, South Korea, China, Taiwan
- **Sort** gained Most favourited, Oldest releases and Title Z–A alongside the existing five.

All of these flow from the URL (`?status=RELEASING&source=MANGA&country=JP&sort=FAVOURITES_DESC`) through Zod validation into the AniList GraphQL query, so filtered views stay shareable and reload correctly. They're wired into the desktop filter row, the mobile filter sheet and its active count in [anime-filters.tsx](components/anime-filters.tsx), and into the removable "Active filters" chips in [discover-browser.tsx](features/anime/discover-browser.tsx).

**Other improvements in the same area**
- Five extra one-tap presets: Fan Favourites, Airing Now, Upcoming, OVAs & Specials and Light Novels. The preset row now wraps on desktop instead of hiding pills off-screen.
- Switching presets resets every filter dimension, including the new ones.
- The degraded/Kitsu fallback cache key now includes the filter set, so during an AniList outage a filtered search can no longer be served results from a different filter set ([catalog-service.ts](services/catalog/catalog-service.ts)).

**Verification**
- `npm run typecheck` passes.
- Focused suite: 63 tests across validation, AniList client and catalogue outage resilience pass, including a new round-trip test for the added filters.
- ESLint is clean on all changed files. The repo-wide `npm run lint` still fails on pre-existing errors in `features/stats/stats-view.tsx`, `features/schedule/*` and `features/settings/settings-view.tsx` — untouched by this change.
- I queried the live AniList GraphQL API to confirm every enum value and the query shape. That also exposed a provider quirk: AniList returns zero results when `status` or `countryOfOrigin` are sent as explicit `null`, so the client omits unset filters rather than sending nulls — verified working across status, source, country, combined filters and the new sorts.

Not run: `next build` or a browser walkthrough of the filter UI; the change was verified by typecheck, lint and provider-level query tests.

The natural next step is letting Discover hide titles you've already tracked, so browsing surfaces new material.
